<date>2026-11-06</date>
<releaseinfo>0.2.0</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>&kappname; (<command>tdeflatpak</command>) is a graphical tool for
the Trinity Desktop Environment (TDE) that installs, updates and removes
Flatpak applications, lets you browse the application catalog, shows
application details, changes what applications may access, and manages
Flatpak remotes.</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>TDE</keyword>
<keyword>Trinity</keyword>
<keyword>Flatpak</keyword>
<keyword>Flathub</keyword>
<keyword>tdeflatpak</keyword>
<keyword>software</keyword>
</keywordset>

</bookinfo>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para><ulink url="https://flatpak.org">Flatpak</ulink> is a system for
distributing desktop applications on Linux. Applications run in a sandbox
and bring their own libraries in shared <emphasis>runtimes</emphasis>, so
they work on many distributions. Applications and runtimes are downloaded
from <emphasis>remotes</emphasis> (repositories). The best known remote is
<ulink url="https://flathub.org">Flathub</ulink>.</para>

<para>&kappname; is a native TDE front end for Flatpak. It uses the
<application>libflatpak</application> library directly, the same way
<application>GNOME Software</application> and Plasma
<application>Discover</application> do, and it can:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>list, start and uninstall installed applications and runtimes;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>search the catalogs of all remotes and install applications;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>check for and install updates;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>add, enable, disable and remove remotes, and add Flathub with one click;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>remove runtimes that are no longer used;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>open <filename>.flatpakref</filename> and <filename>.flatpakrepo</filename> files.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<important>
<title>Disclaimer</title>
<para>This application was largely <quote>vibe coded</quote> with the help
of an AI coding assistant. It has not been thoroughly reviewed or tested,
may contain bugs and can modify the Flatpak installations on your system.
It is provided as-is, without any warranty. Use it at your own risk.</para>
</important>

<sect1 id="installations">
<title>User and System Installations</title>

<para>Flatpak keeps two separate <emphasis>installations</emphasis>:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>System</term>
<listitem><para>Located in <filename>/var/lib/flatpak</filename> and
shared by all users. Changing it requires administrator authorization,
which is requested through <application>polkit</application>. A polkit
authentication agent such as <application>polkit-agent-tde</application>
must be running in your session for the password dialog to appear.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>User</term>
<listitem><para>Located in <filename>~/.local/share/flatpak</filename>
and only available to you. No password is needed.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>&kappname; shows both installations at once. The
<guilabel>Installation</guilabel> column tells you which one an item
belongs to.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="using">
<title>Using &kappname;</title>

<para>Start &kappname; from the TDE menu
(<menuchoice><guimenu>System</guimenu><guimenuitem>Flatpak Manager</guimenuitem></menuchoice>)
or by running <command>tdeflatpak</command>. The main window has five
tabs.</para>

<sect1 id="browse-tab">
<title>The Browse Tab</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Browsing popular Flathub applications</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="browse.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Browse tab</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>The Browse tab is an app store view of the catalog. Choose a section
on the left. The application icons are shown on the right; a check mark
means the application is already installed. Type in the
<guilabel>Filter</guilabel> field to narrow the view.</para>

<para>The categories (<guilabel>Audio &amp; Video</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Games</guilabel>, <guilabel>Graphics &amp; Photography</guilabel>
and so on) come from the AppStream data of all enabled remotes, so they also
work offline and for repositories other than Flathub. Applications without a
main category are listed under <guilabel>Other</guilabel>.</para>

<para>If Flathub is configured and the Flathub web service is enabled (see
<xref linkend="preferences"/>), the sections <guilabel>Popular</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Trending</guilabel>, <guilabel>New</guilabel> and
<guilabel>Recently Updated</guilabel> show the lists that the Flathub
website shows.</para>

<para>Double-click an application, or select it and press
<guibutton>Details...</guibutton>, to open its details (see
<xref linkend="details"/>). <guibutton>Install</guibutton> installs the
selected applications. The context menu offers the same actions.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="installed-tab">
<title>The Installed Tab</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Installed tab</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="installed.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Installed tab</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>This tab lists every installed application with its name, ID,
version, branch, origin remote, installation and disk usage. Type in the
<guilabel>Filter</guilabel> field to narrow the list. Check
<guilabel>Show runtimes</guilabel> to include runtimes and extensions as
well.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Run</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Starts the selected application. Double-clicking an
application does the same.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Details...</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Shows the details of the selected application (see
<xref linkend="details"/>).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Permissions...</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Opens the permission editor for the selected application
(see <xref linkend="permissions"/>).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Uninstall</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Removes the selected items after asking for
confirmation. You can select several items at once. If an uninstalled
application left personal data in <filename>~/.var/app</filename>,
&kappname; offers to delete it too (you can change this in the
preferences).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Refresh</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Reloads the list, for example after you changed
something with the <command>flatpak</command> command line tool.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The context menu of an application also has <guimenuitem>Block
Updates</guimenuitem>. A blocked application stays at its current version
until you allow updates again; this is what <command>flatpak mask</command>
does. For a runtime, <guimenuitem>Keep When Unused (Pin)</guimenuitem>
stops <guimenuitem>Remove Unused Runtimes</guimenuitem> from removing it
(<command>flatpak pin</command>). The <guilabel>Notes</guilabel> column
shows blocked and pinned items.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="search-tab">
<title>The Search &amp; Install Tab</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Searching for applications</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="search.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Search &amp; Install tab</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>When you open this tab for the first time, &kappname; loads the
application catalogs (AppStream data) of all enabled remotes. Catalogs
older than one day are downloaded again automatically. Use
<guibutton>Refresh Catalog</guibutton> to force a download.</para>

<para>Type one or more words in the <guilabel>Search</guilabel> field.
An application matches if every word appears in its name, ID or summary.
At most 300 results are shown, so refine your search if needed.</para>

<para>Select one or more applications and press
<guibutton>Install</guibutton>. Double-click an application, or press
<guibutton>Details...</guibutton>, to see its details first. Each
application is installed into the installation that its remote belongs
to, together with any runtimes it needs. Applications that are already
installed are marked <guilabel>Installed</guilabel>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="updates-tab">
<title>The Updates Tab</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Available updates</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="updates.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Updates tab</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>The first time you open this tab, &kappname; checks all remotes
for updates. The number of available updates is shown in the tab title.
<guibutton>Check for Updates</guibutton> checks again.</para>

<para>Uncheck the items you do not want to update and press
<guibutton>Update Selected</guibutton>, or press
<guibutton>Update All</guibutton>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="remotes-tab">
<title>The Remotes Tab</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Configured remotes</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="remotes.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Remotes tab</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>This tab lists the configured remotes of both installations.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Add...</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Opens the <guilabel>Add Remote</guilabel> dialog,
described below.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Add Flathub</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Adds the Flathub remote. You are asked whether to add it
system-wide or only for you.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Disable</guibutton> / <guibutton>Enable</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>A disabled remote is kept but not used for searching,
installing or updating.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Remove</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Deletes the selected remote. Flatpak refuses to do this
while applications installed from it are still present.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Remove Unused...</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Uninstalls runtimes and extensions that are no longer
needed by any installed application, in both installations.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Add Remote dialog</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="add-remote.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Add Remote dialog</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>In the <guilabel>Add Remote</guilabel> dialog, enter the
<guilabel>Location</guilabel> of a <filename>.flatpakrepo</filename> file
(a local file or a URL). This is the recommended way, because the file
contains the repository's signing key. You can also enter the plain URL of
a Flatpak repository. In that case, leave <guilabel>Verify GPG
signatures</guilabel> enabled unless you know the repository is
unsigned. Choose a <guilabel>Name</guilabel> and the
<guilabel>Installation</guilabel> the remote should be added to.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="details">
<title>Application Details</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Details of an application</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="details.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The application details dialog</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>The details dialog shows the application's description, screenshots
(use the arrow buttons to go through them), license, categories, sizes,
runtime, the latest releases and links to the project's website, bug
tracker and donation page.</para>

<para>Under <guilabel>Permissions</guilabel> it lists what the application
may access. Permissions that weaken the sandbox considerably, such as access
to all your files, to the X11 display or to the full session bus, are
highlighted. For installed applications these are the permissions of the
installed version; use <guibutton>Permissions...</guibutton> to change
them.</para>

<para>The information comes from the AppStream data of the remote. For
Flathub applications, &kappname; also asks the Flathub web service for the
download and installed size, the number of installs and the requested
permissions, if this is enabled in the preferences.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="permissions">
<title>Application Permissions</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The permission editor</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="permissions.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The permission editor</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>Flatpak applications run in a sandbox. Each application requests the
permissions it needs; the permission editor lets you take permissions away
or grant additional ones. Open it with
<menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Application Permissions...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>,
the <guibutton>Permissions...</guibutton> button of an installed
application, or in the Trinity Control Center (see
<xref linkend="kcontrol"/>).</para>

<para>Select an application on the left. <guilabel>All
Applications</guilabel> changes the permissions of every application at
once; its check boxes have a third state that means <quote>no
change</quote>. Changes made by you are shown in bold. The changes apply to
your user account only and are stored in
<filename>~/.local/share/flatpak/overrides</filename>, exactly like
<command>flatpak override --user</command>. They take effect the next time
the application starts. <guibutton>Reset to Defaults</guibutton> removes all
your changes for the selected application.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Permissions</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Network access, display (X11, Wayland), sound, graphics
acceleration, devices, Bluetooth, the session and system bus, the SSH and
GnuPG agents, printing and advanced features.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Files</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Folders the application may access. <guibutton>Add...</guibutton>
grants access to a folder: a special folder such as
<literal>xdg-download</literal>, <literal>home</literal> or
<literal>host</literal>, or a path such as <filename>~/Games</filename>,
read/write, read-only or created if missing. <guibutton>Remove</guibutton>
removes a folder you added, or revokes access that the application
requested.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Environment &amp; D-Bus</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Environment variables set inside the sandbox, and the
D-Bus names the application may talk to, own or see.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Portals</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Decisions you made when the application asked through a
desktop portal, for example to show notifications, run in the background or
use the camera. Revoking one makes the application ask again. These are
stored in the portal permission store, as shown by
<command>flatpak permission-show</command>; changes here take effect
immediately.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Storage &amp; Updates</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The folder with the application's settings and data
(<filename>~/.var/app/<replaceable>application-id</replaceable></filename>)
and its size. <guibutton>Delete Data...</guibutton> removes it, together
with the portal permissions, which resets the application to its first-start
state. The check boxes block updates of the application in the system-wide
or the user installation. These actions take effect immediately.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="preferences">
<title>Preferences</title>

<para>Open the preferences with
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure Flatpak Manager...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
or in the Trinity Control Center.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Install Flatpak files</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Whether applications from <filename>.flatpakref</filename>
files are installed system-wide, for you only, or whether &kappname;
asks every time.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>After uninstalling an application</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Whether the application's data is kept, deleted, or
whether &kappname; asks.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Check for updates every</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>While &kappname; is running it checks for updates in the
background at this interval and shows the number of updates on the
Updates tab.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Download the catalog again after</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How old the AppStream data of a remote may be before it is
downloaded again.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Use the Flathub web service</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Enables the Popular, Trending, New and Recently Updated
lists and the additional details of Flathub applications. Flathub then
learns which applications you look at. The responses are cached;
<guibutton>Clear Cached Data</guibutton> deletes the cache.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="kcontrol">
<title>The Control Center Module</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Flatpak Applications module</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="kcm.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Flatpak Applications module in the Trinity Control Center</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>The Trinity Control Center has a <guilabel>Flatpak
Applications</guilabel> module with the preferences on the
<guilabel>General</guilabel> tab and the permission editor on the
<guilabel>Permissions</guilabel> tab. Start it on its own with
<command>tdecmshell tdeflatpak-settings</command>.
<guibutton>Open Flatpak Manager</guibutton> starts &kappname; for installing,
updating and removing applications. A running &kappname; reads changed
preferences when you open its own configuration dialog or restart it.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="operations">
<title>The Progress Dialog</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>An operation in progress</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="operation.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The progress dialog</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>Installing, updating and uninstalling are shown in a progress
dialog. It reports the current step, the overall progress and a detailed
log. Press <guibutton>Cancel</guibutton> to stop the operation. When it is
finished, the button changes to <guibutton>Close</guibutton>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="files">
<title>Opening Flatpak Files</title>

<para>Websites often offer applications as <filename>.flatpakref</filename>
files and repositories as <filename>.flatpakrepo</filename> files.
&kappname; is registered for both file types, so clicking such a file in
<application>Konqueror</application> opens it. You can also use
<menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Open Flatpak File...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>For a <filename>.flatpakref</filename> file you are asked
whether to install the application system-wide or only for you. Any
remote the application needs is added automatically.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>For a <filename>.flatpakrepo</filename> file, the
<guilabel>Add Remote</guilabel> dialog opens with the file already
filled in.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="commands">
<title>Command Reference</title>

<sect1 id="menus">
<title>Menus</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>O</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Open Flatpak File...</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Opens a <filename>.flatpakref</filename> or
<filename>.flatpakrepo</filename> file.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>Q</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Quit</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Quits &kappname;.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F5</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Refresh</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Reloads the installed applications and remotes.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Check for Updates</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Switches to the Updates tab and checks for updates.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Refresh Application Catalog</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Downloads the application catalogs of all remotes again.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Add Flathub Remote...</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Adds the Flathub remote.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Remove Unused Runtimes...</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Uninstalls runtimes that are no longer used.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Application Permissions...</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Opens the permission editor (see <xref linkend="permissions"/>).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure Flatpak Manager...</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Opens the preferences (see <xref linkend="preferences"/>).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Open in Control Center</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Opens the <guilabel>Flatpak Applications</guilabel> module
(see <xref linkend="kcontrol"/>).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The <guimenu>Help</guimenu> menu is the standard TDE help menu.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="command-line">
<title>Command Line Options</title>

<cmdsynopsis>
<command>tdeflatpak</command>
<group choice="opt"><option>--browse</option></group>
<group choice="opt"><option>--updates</option></group>
<group choice="opt"><option>--search</option></group>
<group choice="opt"><option>--remotes</option></group>
<arg choice="opt"><option>--details</option> <replaceable>app-id</replaceable></arg>
<arg choice="opt"><option>--permissions</option> <replaceable>app-id</replaceable></arg>
<arg choice="opt"><replaceable>file</replaceable></arg>
</cmdsynopsis>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--browse</option></term>
<listitem><para>Start with the Browse tab.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--updates</option></term>
<listitem><para>Start with the Updates tab.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--search</option></term>
<listitem><para>Start with the Search &amp; Install tab.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--remotes</option></term>
<listitem><para>Start with the Remotes tab.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--details</option> <replaceable>app-id</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Show the details of an application, for example
<literal>org.gimp.GIMP</literal>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--permissions</option> <replaceable>app-id</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Open the permission editor for an installed
application.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><replaceable>file</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>A <filename>.flatpakref</filename> or
<filename>.flatpakrepo</filename> file (path or URL) to open.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The generic TDE and TQt options are also accepted; see
<command>tdeflatpak --help-all</command>.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="faq">
<title>Questions and Answers</title>

<qandaset id="faqlist">
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Nothing happens or I get <quote>not authorized</quote>
when installing system-wide.</para></question>
<answer><para>System-wide changes require a polkit authentication agent.
Install <application>polkit-agent-tde</application> and log in again, or
use the user installation instead.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Loading the catalog fails with <quote>Can't pull from
untrusted non-gpg verified remote</quote>.</para></question>
<answer><para>The remote is stored without GPG verification. Flatpak
refuses to download from such a remote into the system installation.
Version 0.1.0 of &kappname; had this problem when adding a remote from a
<filename>.flatpakrepo</filename> file, including <guibutton>Add
Flathub</guibutton>. Add the remote again in the same way, with the same
name and installation: the existing remote is then updated and repaired.
Alternatively, run <userinput><command>sudo flatpak remote-modify
--gpg-verify</command> <replaceable>name</replaceable></userinput>.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The Search tab says that no remotes are configured.</para></question>
<answer><para>Add a remote on the Remotes tab, for example with
<guibutton>Add Flathub</guibutton>, then press
<guibutton>Refresh Catalog</guibutton>.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>File dialogs in Flatpak applications look wrong or do not
open.</para></question>
<answer><para>Flatpak applications use the XDG desktop portals for file
dialogs and similar services. Install
<application>xdg-desktop-portal-trinity</application> so that they use TDE
dialogs.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>An application I just installed is not in the TDE menu.</para></question>
<answer><para>Flatpak exports menu entries to
<filename>/var/lib/flatpak/exports/share</filename> and
<filename>~/.local/share/flatpak/exports/share</filename>. These directories
must be in <envar>XDG_DATA_DIRS</envar>, which the Debian
<application>flatpak</application> package sets up at login. Log out and
in again after installing Flatpak for the first time.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</chapter>

